# Ruby on Rails coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern Ruby
(3.x) idiom and Rails conventions in one pack (this pack has no separate
`lang:ruby` base to extend). Applies only to `*.rb` files.

## Naming and structure

- `snake_case` for methods, local variables, file names, and DB
  columns/tables; `CamelCase` for classes and modules — never mix Ruby's
  own convention with a borrowed one from another language.
- A predicate method (returns true/false) ends in `?` (`published?`, not
  `is_published`); a destructive/bang method that mutates in place or
  raises instead of returning nil ends in `!` and normally has a
  non-bang counterpart (`save!` alongside `save`).
- Two-space indentation, no tabs; keep a method short enough to read on
  one screen — a model method past ~10 lines is a signal to extract a
  private method, a concern, or (per `rules/patterns.mdc`) a service
  object, not to keep growing in place.
- Follow the app's own file layout: `app/models`, `app/controllers`,
  `app/jobs`, `app/services` (if the app already has one) — do not
  invent a new top-level directory for one class.

## Modern Ruby (3.x) idiom

- Reach for `case/in` pattern matching when destructuring a
  hash/array-shaped value (parsed JSON, a multi-branch conditional on a
  value's shape) reads clearer than a chain of `if`/`elsif` or
  `dig`/`fetch` calls; do not force pattern matching onto a simple
  `if value.nil?` check where it adds no clarity.
- An endless method (`def full_name = "#{first_name} #{last_name}"`) is
  fine for a genuine one-expression method; do not squeeze a
  multi-statement method onto one line just to use the syntax.
- Keyword arguments (`def initialize(name:, email:)`) for a method with
  more than one or two parameters, especially when some are optional —
  clearer at the call site than positional args, and safer against
  argument-order mistakes than a trailing options hash.
- Prefer `Data.define`/a plain `Struct` for a small immutable value
  object over a full `ActiveRecord`-backed model when nothing needs
  persisting.
- Safe navigation (`user&.profile&.bio`) only where `nil` is an expected,
  legitimate state for the chain; do not use it to paper over a case
  where a `nil` actually signals a bug that should raise or be handled
  explicitly.

## Frozen string literals and immutability

- New Ruby files start with `# frozen_string_literal: true` (match the
  project's own convention if it already has one) — string literals are
  frozen by default, so any `String#<<`/`gsub!`/`concat` mutation must
  target an explicit `.dup`/`String.new`, not a literal.
- Prefer returning a new value over mutating an argument in place, except
  for methods whose name signals mutation (`!`-suffixed, or a
  conventionally mutating method like `each`'s block).

## Formatting and linting

- Match whatever the project's own `.rubocop.yml` enforces; do not
  hand-format around a configured linter or introduce a style the
  project's cops would flag.
- Run `bundle exec rubocop` (scoped to changed files is fine during
  iteration) before considering a change formatted correctly.
